@meta
id: 07-06
title: Climbing back up a block at a time
chapter: 07
type: lesson
skills: paragraph-motion, goto-line
par: 68
minutes: 6
vscode: scroll to the bottom, then five careful clicks on the way back up
open: INCIDENT-2026-08-14.md

@brief
Someone pasted the tail of the incident timeline out of the on-call channel
and the leading timestamps were eaten. The last five entries in this 97 line
document each need their time putting back at the front of the entry.

From the bottom of the file upwards, they are:

    11:12    the note to support
    10:30    incident closed
    10:15    finance confirmed no negative balances
    10:07    all duplicates reversed
    09:41    first batch reconciled

Every other entry already has its time and must not be touched.

Start at the end of the file and work upwards. Going down and counting would
mean knowing which entry is the seventeenth, and you do not.

@keys
| `G` | last line of the file |
| `{` | back to the previous blank line |
| `j` | down a line |
| `I` | insert at the first non-blank of the line |

@hints
`}` went forward a paragraph at a time. Its opposite is on the neighbouring
key and does the same thing in reverse.
--
`{` moves back to the previous blank line. From a line in the middle of an
entry that is the gap above it — so one `{` puts you above the entry you are
in, and a second `{` puts you above the entry before that.
--
The rep is `{` `{` `j` `I`, the time, `Esc`. The first one is different
because `G` has already left you inside the last entry, so it only needs a
single `{`.

@solution
    G { j I 11:12  <Esc>
    { { j I 10:30  <Esc>
    { { j I 10:15  <Esc>
    { { j I 10:07  <Esc>
    { { j I 09:41  <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Fifty-nine keystrokes, thirty of them the times themselves.

`{` and `}` are a pair and they are worth learning as one thing: the block
you are in has a top and a bottom, and these two keys are how you leave by
either end. Neither cares how long the block is.

The doubled `{` in the rep is the part worth understanding rather than
memorising. `{` means "the blank line before this one", and after the first
press you are standing on a blank line — so the second press is what moves
you over an entire entry. Going forwards the same thing happens with `}`.
Once you have noticed it you stop being surprised by it, and `2{` starts to
look like the obvious way to say it. That is the next lesson.

Working bottom to top also solved a problem you have already met: inserting
text does not move anything you have not visited yet. Here the inserts do
not change the line count at all, but the habit is the same one that saved
you in the resolver — when in doubt, edit from the end backwards.

@start INCIDENT-2026-08-14.md
# Incident 2026-08-14 — duplicate refunds

Severity 2. Customer impact lasted 47 minutes. Two hundred and eleven
orders were refunded twice; all duplicate refunds were reversed the same
evening and no customer was left out of pocket.

This document is the timeline as reconstructed from the payment service
logs and the on-call channel. Times are UTC. The remediation items at the
bottom are tracked in ORD-418 and are not repeated here.

## What went well

The rollback was clean and took four minutes from decision to completion.
Nobody argued about fixing forward under pressure.

## What did not

The counter that would have caught this at 08:02 has no alert attached to
it, so the incident was found by a customer rather than by us. That is
the single item worth fixing.

## Timeline

07:58 A routine deploy of the payments service went out. The release
contained the idempotency key change and nothing else of consequence.

08:02 The refund_failed_total counter began climbing. Nobody was paged;
the counter has no alert on it and never has.

08:11 A support agent reported that a customer had been credited twice
for the same cancelled order. The agent raised it in the support channel
rather than the incident channel, and it sat there for six minutes.

08:17 A second and third report arrived. The support lead moved all three
into the incident channel and paged the on-call engineer.

08:19 On-call acknowledged. First hypothesis was a webhook replay from
the payment provider, which has caused something similar before.

08:21 The on-call engineer pulled the last four deploys and put them in
the channel alongside the counter graph.

08:24 A customer posted a screenshot of two identical refund emails
thirty seconds apart, which pinned the behaviour to the refund path.

08:26 The provider dashboard showed no unusual webhook volume. The
hypothesis was dropped.

08:31 On-call noticed that the deploy at 07:58 lined up with the start of
the counter climbing, and pulled the diff.

08:34 The idempotency key was being derived from the order id alone. Two
refunds against the same order within the retry window therefore shared a
key, and the provider treated the second as a new request rather than a
repeat, because the key was being sent on the wrong header.

08:40 Decision to roll back rather than fix forward. The rollback was
started immediately.

08:45 Rollback completed. The refund_failed_total counter flattened
within ninety seconds.

08:49 Incident declared contained. Work moved to identifying every
duplicate refund issued during the window.

08:52 The payments team joined the incident channel and began drafting
the forward fix while finance waited on the duplicate list.

08:57 A first attempt at the query missed refunds issued through the
admin tool, because those rows carry a different source value.

09:04 A query against the refunds table found 211 duplicates across 197
customers. The list was handed to finance.

09:12 Support was given a holding line to use with any customer who
called in about a double credit.

09:22 Finance began reversing the duplicates in batches of fifty. Each
batch was reconciled against the provider before the next one started.

09:33 The provider rate limited the second batch. Batch size was dropped
to twenty five for the rest of the run.

The first batch was reversed and reconciled cleanly. The remaining
three batches were queued behind it.

All 211 duplicate refunds had been reversed. Reconciliation against
the provider showed no remaining discrepancy.

Finance confirmed that no customer balance was left negative and
that no chargeback had been opened in the window.

The incident was closed. On-call handed over to the payments team
with the rollback still in place and the fix scheduled for the morning.

A note went out to support with wording for any customer who asks
why they saw two refunds and then one reversal on their statement.

